You are an automated app generator. Produce a complete, strict specification and scaffolding plan to build a web application called "AI Art Teacher". Follow these requirements exactly and produce output suitable for code generation (backend APIs, data models, UI pages/components, sample API payloads, acceptance criteria, and test cases). Do not add features beyond those listed. Be precise and unambiguous.

1) App Overview
- Name: AI Art Teacher
- Purpose: Allow students to submit artwork, receive structured feedback and grades (realistic mode) or suggestions (creative mode), maintain submission history, enable community discussion, and run monthly challenges with leaderboards.

2) Users and Roles
- Student: registers, logs in, uploads submissions, views feedback/history, joins communities, participates in challenges.
- Teacher/Moderator: reviews submissions, annotates images, adjusts grades, manages challenges and community settings.
- Admin: manages users, communities, and global settings.

3) Authentication & Accounts
- Provide secure signup/login with email + password and optional OAuth providers. Required fields on signup: `name` (string), `age` (integer), `experience_level` (enum: `beginner|intermediate|advanced`).
- `advanced` experience_level unlocks the most detailed feedback mode for that user by default.
- Sessions: JWT-based with refresh tokens. Passwords stored hashed (bcrypt/argon2).

4) Data Models (strict)
- User: { id, name, email, password_hash, age, experience_level, role, created_at }
- Submission: { id, user_id, title, description, mode: enum(`realistic`,`creative`), original_image_url, reference_image_url (nullable), uploaded_at, is_upside_down: bool, metadata: { orientation, width, height, format }, deleted: bool }
- Feedback: { id, submission_id, reviewer_id (nullable for auto feedback), scores: { color_contrast: number0-100, light_shadow: number0-100, symmetry: number0-100, creativity: number0-100|null }, overall_score: number0-100|null, suggestions: text (for creative), comments: [ {id, author_id, text, created_at, referenced_labels: [int]} ], annotated_image_url (nullable), created_at }
- Community/Thread: { id, name, is_public: bool, members: [user_id], created_at }
- Reaction: { id, target_type: enum(`submission`,`comment`), target_id, user_id, reaction_type }
- Challenge: { id, name, month, entries: [submission_id], leaderboard: [ {user_id, score} ], start_at, end_at }

5) File Uploads & Storage
- Accept images JPEG/PNG/WebP up to configurable max (e.g., 25 MB). Store originals and annotated versions. Provide signed URLs for uploads/downloads. Keep reference image optional.

6) Submission Processing & Upside-down Detection (example)
- Example behavior: on upload, run an orientation check. If the image is detected as rotated (>90% confidence), set `is_upside_down` = true and prompt the user: "Detected rotated image — keep orientation as-is or auto-rotate?" The system must never auto-rotate without explicit user confirmation.
- This behavior is illustrative and must preserve user creativity and intent. Always allow the user to keep unconventional orientations (for artistic reasons) and provide an optional field for the user to add a short note explaining the creative choice. Treat orientation prompts as user-facing examples to support interaction rather than mandatory automatic changes.

7) Modes & Prompting
- Two modes: `realistic` and `creative`. If none chosen, default to `realistic`.
- For each mode, use a different LLM prompting template (provide placeholders for `user_experience_level`, `submission_metadata`, and `reference_image_url`). Advanced users receive more granular, technical feedback.

8) Grading and Feedback Rules
- Realistic mode: produce numeric scores (0–100) and an overall percentage. Required score breakdown: `color_contrast`, `light_shadow`, `symmetry`. Overall_score must be computed as weighted average (weights: color_contrast 35%, light_shadow 35%, symmetry 30%). Provide explicit improvement suggestions tied to each low-scoring metric and at least 3 actionable steps.
- Creative mode: provide textual suggestions and ideas (no numeric overall score). Include optional `creativity` score for internal ranking only (do not display as primary grade unless teacher enables it).
- UI must allow collapsing each grading aspect and toggling whether the chatbot's feedback is considered in the final grade. When toggled off, a human reviewer grade overrides the LLM suggestion.

9) UI Layout & Components (strict)
- Main view: left panel is submission history (list of user's past submissions with thumbnails, timestamps, and delete action). Center panel shows selected submission with image viewer and feedback. Right panel shows reviewer tools and community thread feed.
- Submission Card fields: thumbnail, title, mode badge, overall_score (if realistic), created_at, actions: view, edit (metadata), delete.
- Feedback view: collapsible sections for each metric, suggested improvements, and an optional annotated image overlay with numbered callouts. Clicking a number in the callout scrolls to the corresponding comment.

10) Annotated Images & Numbered Labels
- Teachers or the system can generate an annotated image with numbered circles and a separate legend mapping numbers -> comment text. Annotated images must be stored and a URL included on the Feedback record. Comments may reference `referenced_labels` as integers pointing to annotated image callouts.

11) Community Threads & Privacy
- Two thread types: public (any signed-in user can comment/react) and private (only community members or explicitly allowed user_ids). Communities can be created by users; creators become community admins.
- Support reactions and threaded comments. Provide moderation tools (delete comment, ban user from community).

12) Monthly Challenge & Leaderboard
- Implement monthly challenge objects. Users can opt-in their submissions to the challenge. For realistic-mode challenges, rank by `overall_score`. For creative challenges, rank by a configurable combination of `creativity` (LLM-internal) and community votes. Leaderboard must be exportable as CSV.

13) API Endpoints (examples, strict request/response)
- POST /api/auth/signup {name, email, password, age, experience_level} -> 201 { user }
- POST /api/auth/login {email, password} -> 200 { access_token, refresh_token }
- POST /api/submissions {title, description, mode, signed_upload_url} -> 201 { submission }
- GET /api/submissions/:id -> 200 { submission, feedback[] }
- POST /api/submissions/:id/feedback { reviewer_id, scores?, suggestions?, annotated_image_url? } -> 201 { feedback }
- POST /api/communities {name, is_public} -> 201 { community }
- POST /api/challenges/{month}/entries { submission_id } -> 201 { entry }

14) Non-functional Requirements
- Security: secure auth, input validation, image scanning for malware, RBAC enforcement for private threads.
- Privacy: respect community/private settings; do not make private submissions public without explicit opt-in.
- Scalability: store images in object storage (S3-compatible); process orientation and ML tasks asynchronously via job queue.
- Observability: log major events (upload, feedback created, challenge entry) and provide metrics for monthly challenge usage.

15) Acceptance Criteria & Tests
- Unit tests for API endpoints: signup/login, create submission, fetch submission with feedback, opt-in to challenge, create community (public/private), and delete submission.
- Integration tests for file upload flow including orientation detection prompt and user confirmation.
- E2E test: student uploads submission -> automatic LLM feedback generated -> teacher annotates image -> submission appears on leaderboard when opted into challenge.

